---
description: Chrome extension (MV3) — sync, context guard, Flow DOM
globs: Erasezo-extension/**,1.1.2_0/**
---

**Edit `Erasezo-extension/`, then run `npm --prefix erasezo-web run sync`.**
`1.1.2_0/` is a mirror Chrome loads; editing it directly is reverted by the next
sync, and `test/extension.test.js` fails when the two drift.

**A content script outlives its extension.** After a reload, `chrome.storage`
and `chrome.runtime` still exist as objects, so an existence check passes and
the call throws `Extension context invalidated`. Only `chrome.runtime.id`
disappears. `contextGuard.js` runs first in both content-script lists and wraps
the entry points — route new `chrome.*` calls through it rather than guarding
them one by one.

**`page/flowInjector.js` — anything Google-owned is a preference, not a
requirement.** Media URLs have exactly one definition (`MEDIA_ENDPOINT`) covering
all three shapes Flow has served; don't write the pattern a second time. Tile
containers list Google's element names with `isTileLike()` as the structural
fallback, because the current Angular build dropped `data-tile-id` entirely.

Debugging a missing tile button: run `__erasioFlowDiag()` in the Flow tab's
console — it names which of the five gates rejected each tile.

Manifest facts worth not breaking: `contextGuard.js` must stay first in every
`content_scripts.js` array, `options_page` is `user.html` (not the admin panel),
and both `labs.google` and `flow.google.com` are supported.
